WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.327
Week eleven goes inside a media agency.

00:00:03.377 --> 00:00:04.177
First,

00:00:04.203 --> 00:00:05.191
Ali Coysh,

00:00:05.241 --> 00:00:07.290
National Head of Strategy at Initiative,

00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.586
on how media strategy works.

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.979
Fifteen years ago, media, creative and PR agencies sat around the client.

00:00:15.029 --> 00:00:18.666
Now it's crowded with analytics and in-house teams too.

00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:20.720
Four things drove that:

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:21.652
more channels,

00:00:21.702 --> 00:00:22.973
the rise of specialists,

00:00:23.023 --> 00:00:24.470
economic pressure on budgets,

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:25.320
and a data boom,

00:00:24.662 --> 00:00:27.469
along with privacy laws.

00:00:28.167 --> 00:00:29.921
So who owns strategy?

00:00:29.971 --> 00:00:32.917
Creative and media agencies used to work together.

00:00:32.967 --> 00:00:36.653
Now they compete for it, both pitching to the same client.

00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:39.381
Media's job has grown too.

00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:42.930
It used to be planning, buying and reporting on paid media.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:48.301
Now it orchestrates experiences across owned, earned, shared and paid.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.346
To choose channels, strategists weigh eight inputs.

00:00:52.396 --> 00:00:53.443
Who's the audience,

00:00:53.493 --> 00:00:54.481
what do they watch,

00:00:54.531 --> 00:00:55.872
what's happening in culture,

00:00:55.922 --> 00:00:58.086
and where do they shop the category?

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:00.283
Then the hard numbers.

00:01:00.333 --> 00:01:02.064
Which channel works best?

00:01:02.114 --> 00:01:04.505
What does modelling say drives return?

00:01:04.555 --> 00:01:08.222
Which markets matter, and what creative assets are there?

00:01:08.933 --> 00:01:11.454
Every decision follows one logic flow.

00:01:11.504 --> 00:01:12.562
Journey stage,

00:01:12.612 --> 00:01:13.412
then the bond or barrier,

00:01:12.766 --> 00:01:14.697
the media role,

00:01:14.747 --> 00:01:15.593
and then channel,

00:01:15.643 --> 00:01:18.043
platform and the exact environment.

00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:20.145
Take Lego City.

00:01:20.195 --> 00:01:23.447
Kids felt it was losing out to newer, cooler toys.

00:01:23.497 --> 00:01:27.531
So Lego partnered with Nitro Circus and reignited the love.

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:30.825
Crown wasn't seen as a dining destination.

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:33.077
So it sponsored MasterChef on TV,

00:01:33.127 --> 00:01:36.623
rebuilding its dining credentials where food lovers were watching.

00:01:37.333 --> 00:01:40.844
In Queensland, locals saw NRMA as an outsider.

00:01:40.894 --> 00:01:43.757
So it teamed up with the Courier Mail on Help our highway,

00:01:43.807 --> 00:01:46.251
campaigning to fix the Bruce Highway.

00:01:46.967 --> 00:01:47.943
It worked.

00:01:47.993 --> 00:01:52.294
NRMA hit its best non-customer consideration in Queensland in six years,

00:01:52.344 --> 00:01:55.837
and the government committed four hundred and sixty-seven million dollars.

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:57.450
Ali's answer to creative versus media?

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.846
Creative and media.

00:02:00.896 --> 00:02:05.955
Insight, creativity, collaboration, and human thinking plus AI.

00:02:06.667 --> 00:02:11.322
Then Geoff Clarke, Chief Operating Officer of IPG Mediabrands Australia.

00:02:11.372 --> 00:02:15.261
ChatGPT reached about seven hundred million users in under two years,

00:02:15.311 --> 00:02:16.346
so regulation,

00:02:16.396 --> 00:02:19.377
ethics and education are playing catch-up.

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:21.727
Here's the false economy.

00:02:21.777 --> 00:02:24.274
Upstream work is now heavily AI driven,

00:02:24.324 --> 00:02:25.288
but downstream,

00:02:25.338 --> 00:02:26.833
seventy percent of the workload,

00:02:26.883 --> 00:02:28.865
is still highly manual.

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:31.522
So agencies built bots.

00:02:31.572 --> 00:02:33.302
Trigger bots react to an email,

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:35.130
automatic bots take over next,

00:02:35.180 --> 00:02:37.371
and schedule bots run at set times.

00:02:37.421 --> 00:02:40.453
Geoff calls them an invisible police force.

00:02:41.167 --> 00:02:45.292
Bots now run TV setup, bookings and budget reconciliation.

00:02:45.342 --> 00:02:46.317
With offshoring,

00:02:46.367 --> 00:02:47.402
they saved about two hundred and seven thousand hours in five years,

00:02:47.452 --> 00:02:51.645
and clients became partners.

00:02:52.333 --> 00:02:53.887
So what skills matter?

00:02:53.937 --> 00:02:58.250
Data-driven thinking, creative and strategic thinking, and adaptability.

00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:01.251
The lasting edge is the craft of your people.

00:03:01.967 --> 00:03:02.767
So:

00:03:02.663 --> 00:03:04.629
media orchestrates experiences,

00:03:04.679 --> 00:03:06.398
choices follow the logic flow,

00:03:06.448 --> 00:03:07.967
and bots take the grind.

00:03:08.017 --> 00:03:12.070
Use AI to free time for high-value work, and lead the change.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:13.909
That's week eleven.
